{"canonical_record":{"canonical_payload_sha256":"84ed26e5ee20f620e9838462c65d85a3a74cd95e482d0d82c04d00f42d8a75d6","full_review_record_id":"fullrev_18ca1d3d610f4210061687e94f8daf7ace93deaf92399716dc9ace110e49e38d","overall_assessment":"The paper presents a novel methodological contribution by defining a graph convolution operator based on the expected number of visits of a random walk, which enables weight sharing across nodes in graph-structured data. The work is positioned clearly against existing spectral and clustering-based approaches, highlighting the advantage of handling varying graph structures. The experimental evaluation on MNIST and Merck molecular activity data demonstrates the feasibility of the approach, though the scope is limited to shallow architectures and specific datasets.","paper_id":"BkIqod5ll","paper_identity_sha256":"5876779271625a6de1aa20a96674bcc156e13711d9ed5d196722eec0295fc268","prompt_identity_sha256":"f73dd00f83178fd8500acb611769ea43d80237f7571e86a665f57ddf72c7afda","raw_response_sha256":"81a701045a800728a067e4bc3ffcddede9573d70c47bff95ce895f55975cfd1f","request_identity_sha256":"24517a1db902f266c148b68dde60afcc389c4fe80512fd32c9bebf72b2dbd2b7","reviewer_identity_sha256":"32b29c26fbf1508e066dfb25b1a9a3fe0654c5869a7da3020e1e8e61940befe3","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"agentreview-full-review-corpus-v4","structural_context_sha256":"6c3590d1447a48c304ffdda6559d30babd11b5189ab56dea457273eae566f669","subdimension_sections":[{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-004","SRC-006"],"review_text":"The paper does not introduce a fundamentally new problem or dataset. Instead, it frames the application of CNNs to graph-structured data as a generalization of existing supervised learning tasks. The authors claim that standard regression and classification problems can be treated as graph-structured data by learning the graph structure from the correlation matrix. While this framing is useful for unifying different data types under a single framework, the problem formulation itself is an extension of existing tasks rather than a novel problem definition.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-PROB"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-005","SRC-009","SRC-012","SRC-014"],"review_text":"The paper introduces a novel convolution operator for graphs that utilizes the expected number of visits of a random walk to select neighbors. This approach allows for weight sharing across nodes, which is a significant departure from previous methods that either did not share weights or were limited to fixed-size graphs. The method is presented as a generalization of standard CNNs to graph-structured data, with the convolution defined as an inner product of weights and the selected top neighbors in a preference order. This represents a clear algorithmic advance in the field of graph neural networks.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-MTH"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-013","SRC-015","SRC-018"],"review_text":"The paper provides an analysis of the properties of the proposed convolution operator, including its behavior as the power of the transition matrix increases. The authors note that for large values of k, local information is smoothed out, and the convolution is applied repeatedly to features with maximum connections. This analysis offers insight into the trade-offs between local and global information in the graph structure. However, the theoretical depth is limited, and the analysis is primarily focused on the practical implications of the method rather than providing a comprehensive theoretical framework.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-ANL"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-021","SRC-022","SRC-027"],"review_text":"The experimental setup uses well-known benchmarks, namely the Merck molecular activity challenge and the MNIST dataset. The authors do not introduce new datasets or evaluation protocols but rather apply the proposed method to existing benchmarks. The experiments are designed to demonstrate the feasibility of the method and compare it to state-of-the-art models. While the use of established benchmarks is appropriate for validating the method, the lack of new experimental setups or datasets limits the originality in this subdimension.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-EXP"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-009","SRC-019"],"review_text":"The paper combines the concept of random walks on graphs with the convolutional neural network architecture. The rationale for this combination is that the random walk provides a natural way to select neighbors based on the graph structure, which is then used to define the convolution. This combination is presented as a novel approach to handling graph-structured data, and the authors argue that it preserves locality while being applicable to different graph structures. The combination is well-articulated and provides a clear justification for its novelty.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-COM"},{"paper_anchor_ids":["SRC-026"],"review_text":"The paper does not present negative results or critical assessments of existing methods. Instead, it focuses on demonstrating the performance of the proposed method and comparing it to state-of-the-art models. The authors acknowledge that the pooling method tested does not outperform other methods, but this is presented as a limitation rather than a critical assessment. The paper does not provide a systematic analysis of the limitations of existing methods or negative results that challenge assumptions in the field.","subdimension_id":"ORIG-NEG"}]},"disposition":"CANONICAL_FULL_REVIEW_COMMITTED","item_commit_sha256":"63ebd05fd8fe36aae325e700938eb98d422569d6cc7c29e88ec48252e36cd675","paper_id":"BkIqod5ll","paper_ordinal":48,"raw_attempts":[{"assistant_content_base64":"","assistant_content_sha256":"81a701045a800728a067e4bc3ffcddede9573d70c47bff95ce895f55975cfd1f","attempt_index":0,"error":"","finish_reason":"stop","paper_id":"BkIqod5ll","raw_provider_response_base64":"","raw_provider_response_sha256":"d818594e59a893acd4abd7d4b561797d41fd45bb9a0b5ce4f15633ac57375ba1","request_identity_sha256":"24517a1db902f266c148b68dde60afcc389c4fe80512fd32c9bebf72b2dbd2b7","role":"ORIG","schema_version":"no-memory-full-review-corpus-build-v6","usage":{"completion_tokens":1079,"prompt_tokens":15983,"prompt_tokens_details":null,"total_tokens":17062},"valid":true}],"request_identity_sha256":"24517a1db902f266c148b68dde60afcc389c4fe80512fd32c9bebf72b2dbd2b7","role":"ORIG","role_ordinal":3,"schema_version":"corpus-build-bootstrap-checkpoint-v4"}